--- body ---
## Context
After tickets 0116 (schema-literal struct) and 0117 (validating-parser
merge), `Erg.BlockedBys` still carries raw strings that the ref grammar
can already parse. Lazy parsing via `BlockedByRefs()` is the last
surviving accessor method after 0116. Push the parse to parse-time;
delete the accessor.

## Reimagined: Created descoped
The original ticket also proposed `Created string → time.Time`. Audit
found only 1 read site (validate.go regex check) and 1 write site
(new.go string interpolation). `Created` is never compared, sorted, or
computed on — it is a display-only field. Converting saves the regex
but adds a `Format("2006-01-02")` call; net wash, plus zero-value
gotchas and import weight. Leaving `Created` as `string`.

## Proposed changes
1. `BlockedBys []string` → `BlockedBys []Ref`. Each ref is parsed at
   parse time; malformed values become errors in `parseErg`'s
   `[]string` return (per 0117). Delete `BlockedByRefs()`.
2. Update the 5 call sites that use `BlockedByRefs()` to read the typed
   field directly:
   - archive.go:63
   - check.go:48
   - close.go:129 (uses raw strings for line manipulation — preserved
     via `Ref.Raw`)
   - ready.go:243
   - validate.go: any remaining corpus-level ref users

## Out of scope
- `Created` field stays `string` (descoped — see log entry).
- Other field types stay as designed by 0116.

## Dependencies
- Hard dep: ticket 0117 (validating-parser merge). The parse-time error
  channel from 0117 is where malformed refs flow as errors.

## Plan (commits)

**C1: Change field type + update parser.**
- `BlockedBys []Line` → `BlockedBys []Ref` in Erg struct.
- In `parseErgBytes`: parse each Blocked-by value via `parseRef` at
  append time. Move rule 9 error emission from end-of-walk loop
  (erg.go:393-396) into the header-parsing switch case (erg.go:311-312).
  On parse error, emit error AND still append the Ref (Kind=RefInvalid,
  Raw set) so downstream code can iterate uniformly.
- Delete the end-of-walk rule 9 block.

**C2: Update call sites + delete BlockedByRefs().**
- archive.go:74 — `refs, errs := t.BlockedByRefs()` → iterate
  `t.BlockedBys` directly; drop the `errs[j] != nil` guard (malformed
  refs are now RefInvalid, Kind check suffices).
- check.go:48 — same pattern.
- close.go:132 — `for _, r := range t.BlockedBys` currently compares
  raw strings; change to `for _, ref := range t.BlockedBys` and compare
  `ref.Raw == closedID` or `ref.ID == closedID`.
- ready.go:239 — same as archive.go.
- validate.go:23 (detectCycles) — same pattern.
- validate.go:129 (validateCorpus rule 10) — iterate `t.BlockedBys`,
  skip `ref.Kind != RefLocal`.
- validate.go:239 (cmdValidate rule 10) — same pattern.
- Delete `BlockedByRefs()` method from erg.go:80-91.

**C3: Update tests.**
- erg_test.go:107 — `bb := erg.BlockedBys` now returns `[]Ref`;
  assert `len(bb) == 2` and check `bb[0].ID == "0002"`.
- validate_test.go:368 — `refs, refErrs := erg.BlockedByRefs()` →
  iterate `erg.BlockedBys` directly.

**First test:** after C1, `go build` must compile (no stale references)
and `make test` must pass. After C2, `make test` must pass with
BlockedByRefs() deleted (any stale reference → compile error).

## Exit criteria
- `Erg.BlockedBys` is `[]Ref`.
- `BlockedByRefs()` method deleted; call sites read `t.BlockedBys`
  directly.
- Malformed refs produce parse errors via 0117's mechanism.
- `make test` passes.
